          "answer": "Based on `src/inventory/reorder.py`, here's how the reorder logic works:\n\n**Two functions determine what and how much to order:**\n\n1. **`reorder_due()`** \u2014 Returns a list of every SKU whose current stock level is at or below the reorder threshold.\n   - It queries all items via `all_items()`, then checks each item's level with `level_for(item[\"sku\"])`\n   - An item appears in the \"due\" list when `level_for(sku) <= REORDER_THRESHOLD` (where `REORDER_THRESHOLD = 12`)\n   - This is the \"what to order\" list \u2014 every SKU that needs replenishing\n\n2. **`order_size(sku)`** \u2014 Computes how many units to order for a specific SKU.\n   - Formula: `shortfall = REORDER_THRESHOLD * 3 - level_for(sku)`, which simplifies to `36 - current_level`\n   - Then caps the result: `min(max(shortfall, 0), MAX_BATCH)` \u2014 ensuring the order is at least 0 and at most 250 (where `MAX_BATCH = 250`)\n   - **Examples:**\n     - If stock level is 0 \u2192 order 36 units\n     - If stock level is 12 (at threshold) \u2192 order 24 units\n     - If stock level is 20 \u2192 order 16 units\n     - If stock level is 36 or above \u2192 order 0 units (but the SKU would still appear in `reorder_due()` since it's at or below the threshold check... wait, actually if level > 12 it wouldn't appear in `reorder_due()`, so `order_size` would only be called for items that are already flagged)\n\n**In practice:** The workflow appears to be:\n- Run `reorder_due()` to get the list of SKUs needing attention\n- For each SKU on that list, call `order_size(sku)` to determine the quantity to order (capped at 250 max)",
